          "text": "Measles day counts\nThe answered Meyers activity teaches isolation through day 4 after rash onset. Supplied ATI PED244 describes\ncommunicability from 4 days before through 5 days after rash onset. Isolation and communicability wording are not\nsilently merged; follow the actual infection-prevention plan. No check chooses one unresolved day-count\nboundary.\nFifth disease\nThe “usually no longer contagious when rash appears” statement applies to an otherwise healthy child. Persistent\ninfection in immunocompromised children and pregnancy/hemolytic-disease exposure need individualized\nassessment.\nKawasaki aspirin exception\nRoutine pediatric viral-illness aspirin avoidance and prescribed Kawasaki aspirin have different contexts.\nDistinguish febrile anti-inflammatory treatment from later antiplatelet treatment; do not independently apply one\nphase’s dose to another.\nIVIG and vaccines\nThe 11-month MMR/varicella delay is tied to the Kawasaki high-dose IVIG context. Preserve immune-globulin\nproduct/dose and vaccine type; it is not a delay for every vaccine after every infusion.\nReturn and prophylaxis\nSchool exclusion, return to sport, and antibiotic-prophylaxis duration are condition- and plan-specific. Improvement\nin energy does not clear mono contact sport; rheumatic prophylaxis depends on cardiac involvement and\nrecurrence risk.\nUnsupported or unresolved claims\nAn unresolved numerical difference is not used as the deciding boundary of a check. A source-limited topic is\nlabeled below rather than claimed as ATI-supported. Age, formulation, route, dose unit, treatment phase,\nprocedure, and prescribed plan remain attached to their context. No unrelated disorders were added as standalone\nlessons."
