      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "G-L16-s1",
          "title": "Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)",
          "body": "Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR) is backward movement of urine from the bladder toward the ureters/kidneys. It can promote recurrent UTI, pyelonephritis, and renal scarring. The lecture grades severity I-V, with V most severe.",
          "kind": "idea",
          "terms": [
            [
              "VUR",
              "Vesicoureteral reflux: backflow toward ureters/kidneys."
            ],
            [
              "VCUG",
              "Voiding cystourethrogram: imaging used to evaluate urinary abnormalities/reflux."
            ],
            [
              "Renal scarring",
              "Kidney tissue damage that can follow recurrent upper-tract infection."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "G-L16-s2",
          "title": "Understand the pattern",
          "body": "Reflux may be identified during evaluation for repeated febrile UTIs. Assess the same age-dependent UTI cues: nonspecific feeding/fever illness in infants, dysuria/urgency/new wetting in older children, and fever with flank pain or vomiting in upper-tract illness.",
          "kind": "idea"
        },
        {
          "id": "G-L16-s3",
          "title": "Connect it to nursing care",
          "body": "Assess urine output, blood pressure, growth, renal function, and previous infections as ordered. Backflow does not mean every child will be anuric, but absent output is an urgent assessment finding.",
          "kind": "idea"
        },
        {
          "id": "G-L16-s4",
          "title": "Connect it to nursing care",
          "body": "A voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) may be ordered in the evaluation of urinary-tract abnormalities or recurrent infection. Explain the prescribed imaging and any required catheterization in age-appropriate language, then follow post-test monitoring. Diagnostic testing evaluates the problem; it does not itself correct reflux.",
          "kind": "idea"
        },
        {
          "id": "G-L16-s5",
          "title": "At the bedside",
          "body": "A child has repeated febrile UTIs. Reflux imaging may investigate why infections recur; the test itself does not correct the backflow.",
          "kind": "case"
        }
      ],
