      "path": "N",
      "subject": "N",
      "title": "OCD: planned therapy changes the loop",
      "focus": "Distinguish initial support from planned response prevention and medication treatment.",
      "topic": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
      "codes": [
        "N05"
      ],
      "page": 53,
      "checkPage": 54,
      "keyPage": 88,
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "N-L17-s1",
          "title": "Understand the pattern",
          "body": "Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) works on relationships among thoughts, distress, and actions. Cognitive reframing examines an unhelpful thought and builds a more balanced alternative. Planned exposure and response prevention (ERP) involves approaching a feared cue while refraining from the usual ritual so anxiety can diminish without it. This is a structured therapeutic approach, not permission for a nurse to abruptly remove rituals without a plan. The supplied ATI text describes response prevention and SSRIs as relevant treatments for OCD.",
          "kind": "idea",
          "terms": [
            [
              "CBT",
              "Cognitive behavioral therapy: work on thought and behavior patterns."
            ],
            [
              "ERP",
              "Exposure and response prevention: planned exposure to a feared cue with prevention of the compulsive response."
            ],
            [
              "SSRI",
              "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor; a medication class, not another name for behavioral therapy."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "N-L17-s2",
          "title": "Connect it to nursing care",
          "body": "Support the agreed therapy, gradual coping practice, and caregiver plan. A prescribed SSRI such as sertraline or fluoxetine may help symptoms; monitor adherence, adverse effects, and mood/suicidal changes, especially during initiation or changes. Do not independently adjust or stop it. Evaluate less ritual time, better anxiety tolerance, healing skin, and return to daily function; temporary distress during planned practice does not by itself establish treatment failure.",
          "kind": "idea"
        },
        {
          "id": "N-L17-s3",
          "title": "At the bedside",
          "body": "The child follows a therapist-designed practice after touching a feared object, uses a coping skill, and delays washing as planned. The nurse supports the plan and observes distress and function rather than inventing a harsher exposure.",
          "kind": "case"
        }
      ],
